# contamination-and-mixing-prevention-as-a-rule-class

A coherent class of rules across the session all enforce a single meta-principle: once a quality class, purity state, or environmental category is established for a lot or container, it must be protected from contamination or mixing with incompatible classes. This class includes bin-category segregation, moisture contamination prevention, MSL integrity enforcement, ESD class matching, and cleanroom-boundary crossing protocols. Rules in this class share a common enforcement posture: physical separation, mandatory remediation before re-use, and hard stops rather than warnings.

## Procedures
- Identify the quality class, purity state, and environmental sensitivity of each lot before any handling step.
- Select containers, routes, and handling procedures that maintain the established class — never downgrade by mixing or exposing to incompatible conditions.
- When a class boundary is crossed (cleanroom class, MSL seal, bin category), apply the mandatory remediation protocol before continuing (UV cycle, bakeout, separate sealed container).
- Treat any suspected cross-contamination as a confirmed violation requiring quarantine and Quality notification until cleared.

## Conventions
- Each rule in this class has its own mandatory remediation path — 'undo' is not generic but prescribed per contamination type.
- The cost of preventing contamination (alternate routes, bakeout time, extra containers, multi-trip transport) is always lower than the cost of a contamination event.

